{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And behold, this one came, etc.</b> And behold, a rider, a man, with a pair of horsemen." "<b>And he answered.</b> Each one." "<b>And he said, Fallen, fallen.</b> Twice, because no survivor remained to it." "<b>Shavar.</b> The one who breaks [it] to the land, like 'who bore him to Levi' (Numbers 26:59)."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {3 "The comparison is to the root ילד in Numbers 26:59, read as a hiphil ('who bore him'); Ibn Ezra reads שבר similarly as a hiphil participle, 'the one who breaks/shatters,' with לארץ as its object."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:54:30.907500879Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}